<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?><feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xml:lang="en"><generator uri="https://jekyllrb.com/" version="4.4.1">Jekyll</generator><link href="https://anangsahroni.github.io/feed.xml" rel="self" type="application/atom+xml"/><link href="https://anangsahroni.github.io/" rel="alternate" type="text/html" hreflang="en"/><updated>2026-11-13T11:32:48+00:00</updated><id>https://anangsahroni.github.io/feed.xml</id><title type="html">blank</title><subtitle>A simple site for me and my articles. </subtitle><entry><title type="html">Baseline correction of accelerometer records</title><link href="https://anangsahroni.github.io/blog/2022/koreksi-baseline-pada-rekaman-akselerometer/" rel="alternate" type="text/html" title="Baseline correction of accelerometer records"/><published>2022-07-18T02:00:00+00:00</published><updated>2022-07-18T02:00:00+00:00</updated><id>https://anangsahroni.github.io/blog/2022/koreksi-baseline-pada-rekaman-akselerometer</id><content type="html" xml:base="https://anangsahroni.github.io/blog/2022/koreksi-baseline-pada-rekaman-akselerometer/"><![CDATA[<div class="jupyter-notebook" style="position: relative; width: 100%; margin: 0 auto;"> <div class="jupyter-notebook-iframe-container"> <iframe src="/assets/jupyter/03_Koreksi_Baseline.ipynb.html" style="position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; border-style: none;" width="100%" height="100%" onload="this.parentElement.style.paddingBottom = (this.contentWindow.document.documentElement.scrollHeight + 10) + 'px'"></iframe> </div> </div>]]></content><author><name></name></author><category term="notebook"/><category term="seismology,"/><category term="python,"/><category term="geophysics"/><summary type="html"><![CDATA[Using Python and ObsPy to correct the baseline of accelerometer records]]></summary></entry><entry><title type="html">Ground-motion simulation with `obspy`</title><link href="https://anangsahroni.github.io/blog/2022/simulasi-gerakan-tanah/" rel="alternate" type="text/html" title="Ground-motion simulation with `obspy`"/><published>2022-07-17T02:00:00+00:00</published><updated>2022-07-17T02:00:00+00:00</updated><id>https://anangsahroni.github.io/blog/2022/simulasi-gerakan-tanah</id><content type="html" xml:base="https://anangsahroni.github.io/blog/2022/simulasi-gerakan-tanah/"><![CDATA[<div class="jupyter-notebook" style="position: relative; width: 100%; margin: 0 auto;"> <div class="jupyter-notebook-iframe-container"> <iframe src="/assets/jupyter/02_Simulasi_Gerakan_Tanah.ipynb.html" style="position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; border-style: none;" width="100%" height="100%" onload="this.parentElement.style.paddingBottom = (this.contentWindow.document.documentElement.scrollHeight + 10) + 'px'"></iframe> </div> </div>]]></content><author><name></name></author><category term="notebook"/><category term="seismology,"/><category term="python,"/><category term="geophysics"/><summary type="html"><![CDATA[Using ObsPy to simulate ground motion (displacement, velocity and acceleration)]]></summary></entry><entry><title type="html">Introduction to `obspy` and simple earthquake signal processing</title><link href="https://anangsahroni.github.io/blog/2022/pengenalan-obspy-dan-pengolahan-sinyal-gempa/" rel="alternate" type="text/html" title="Introduction to `obspy` and simple earthquake signal processing"/><published>2022-07-16T02:00:00+00:00</published><updated>2022-07-16T02:00:00+00:00</updated><id>https://anangsahroni.github.io/blog/2022/pengenalan-obspy-dan-pengolahan-sinyal-gempa</id><content type="html" xml:base="https://anangsahroni.github.io/blog/2022/pengenalan-obspy-dan-pengolahan-sinyal-gempa/"><![CDATA[<div class="jupyter-notebook" style="position: relative; width: 100%; margin: 0 auto;"> <div class="jupyter-notebook-iframe-container"> <iframe src="/assets/jupyter/1_Pengenalan_Obspy_dan_Pengolahan_Sinyal_Sederhana.ipynb.html" style="position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; border-style: none;" width="100%" height="100%" onload="this.parentElement.style.paddingBottom = (this.contentWindow.document.documentElement.scrollHeight + 10) + 'px'"></iframe> </div> </div>]]></content><author><name></name></author><category term="notebook"/><category term="seismology,"/><category term="python,"/><category term="geophysics"/><summary type="html"><![CDATA[Using ObsPy for simple processing of earthquake signals]]></summary></entry><entry><title type="html">Locating an earthquake hypocentre with a probabilistic method</title><link href="https://anangsahroni.github.io/blog/2021/episenter-gempa-probabilistik/" rel="alternate" type="text/html" title="Locating an earthquake hypocentre with a probabilistic method"/><published>2021-09-27T02:00:00+00:00</published><updated>2021-09-27T02:00:00+00:00</updated><id>https://anangsahroni.github.io/blog/2021/episenter-gempa-probabilistik</id><content type="html" xml:base="https://anangsahroni.github.io/blog/2021/episenter-gempa-probabilistik/"><![CDATA[<div class="jupyter-notebook" style="position: relative; width: 100%; margin: 0 auto;"> <div class="jupyter-notebook-iframe-container"> <iframe src="/assets/jupyter/2_Earthquake_Source_Simple_2_2.ipynb.html" style="position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; border-style: none;" width="100%" height="100%" onload="this.parentElement.style.paddingBottom = (this.contentWindow.document.documentElement.scrollHeight + 10) + 'px'"></iframe> </div> </div>]]></content><author><name></name></author><category term="notebook"/><category term="seismology,"/><category term="python,"/><category term="geophysics"/><summary type="html"><![CDATA[Using a probabilistic method to locate an earthquake hypocentre in Python]]></summary></entry><entry><title type="html">Locating an earthquake hypocentre with the Geiger method</title><link href="https://anangsahroni.github.io/blog/2021/episenter-gempa-geiger/" rel="alternate" type="text/html" title="Locating an earthquake hypocentre with the Geiger method"/><published>2021-09-26T02:00:00+00:00</published><updated>2021-09-26T02:00:00+00:00</updated><id>https://anangsahroni.github.io/blog/2021/episenter-gempa-geiger</id><content type="html" xml:base="https://anangsahroni.github.io/blog/2021/episenter-gempa-geiger/"><![CDATA[<div class="jupyter-notebook" style="position: relative; width: 100%; margin: 0 auto;"> <div class="jupyter-notebook-iframe-container"> <iframe src="/assets/jupyter/2_Earthquake_Source_Simple_2_1.ipynb.html" style="position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; border-style: none;" width="100%" height="100%" onload="this.parentElement.style.paddingBottom = (this.contentWindow.document.documentElement.scrollHeight + 10) + 'px'"></iframe> </div> </div>]]></content><author><name></name></author><category term="notebook"/><category term="seismology,"/><category term="python,"/><category term="geophysics"/><summary type="html"><![CDATA[Using the Geiger method to locate an earthquake hypocentre in Python]]></summary></entry><entry><title type="html">Locating an earthquake epicentre with the circle method</title><link href="https://anangsahroni.github.io/blog/2021/episenter-gempa-lingkaran/" rel="alternate" type="text/html" title="Locating an earthquake epicentre with the circle method"/><published>2021-09-25T02:00:00+00:00</published><updated>2021-09-25T02:00:00+00:00</updated><id>https://anangsahroni.github.io/blog/2021/episenter-gempa-lingkaran</id><content type="html" xml:base="https://anangsahroni.github.io/blog/2021/episenter-gempa-lingkaran/"><![CDATA[<div class="jupyter-notebook" style="position: relative; width: 100%; margin: 0 auto;"> <div class="jupyter-notebook-iframe-container"> <iframe src="/assets/jupyter/2_Earthquake_Source_Simple_1.ipynb.html" style="position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; border-style: none;" width="100%" height="100%" onload="this.parentElement.style.paddingBottom = (this.contentWindow.document.documentElement.scrollHeight + 10) + 'px'"></iframe> </div> </div>]]></content><author><name></name></author><category term="notebook"/><category term="seismology,"/><category term="python,"/><category term="geophysics"/><summary type="html"><![CDATA[Using the circle method to locate an earthquake epicentre in Python]]></summary></entry><entry><title type="html">Vertical gravity field of a point mass</title><link href="https://anangsahroni.github.io/blog/2019/medan-gravitasi-vertikl/" rel="alternate" type="text/html" title="Vertical gravity field of a point mass"/><published>2019-05-21T16:00:00+00:00</published><updated>2019-05-21T16:00:00+00:00</updated><id>https://anangsahroni.github.io/blog/2019/medan-gravitasi-vertikl</id><content type="html" xml:base="https://anangsahroni.github.io/blog/2019/medan-gravitasi-vertikl/"><![CDATA[<blockquote> <p>This post is also part of my own learning. If you spot a mistake, please let me know.</p> </blockquote> <p>While reading <a href="https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/gravity-and-magnetic-exploration/20BABB4AAFCDF8DC7F4CB36B8359D482">Gravity and Magnetic Exploration: Principles, Practices, and Applications</a> by William J. Hinze, Ralph R. B. von Frese and Afif H. Saad (2012), I wanted to learn how to map the magnitude of a gravity field with <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Python</code>. The task is fairly simple, but as a beginner I learned a lot by working through it step by step.</p> <p>I hope this is useful for other <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Python</code> beginners, and suggestions from experienced readers are welcome. The code below is simple and contains several simplifications, for example in the derivative, which I hope to improve later.</p> <p>This post computes the gravity field ($ \boldsymbol{g} $) of a point mass $ m $ kg at a depth of $ d $ metres, in the $ x $, $ y $ or $ z $ component, by differentiating the gravitational potential $ U $. The gravitational potential is [1]:</p> \[U = G \frac{m} {r}\] <p>where $ G $ is the gravitational constant, $ m $ the mass and $ r $ the distance, $ \sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2} $. For the $ z $ component [2]:</p> \[\boldsymbol{g_{z}} = \frac {\partial U}{\partial z}\] <p>Here I compute the derivative with the simple definition [3]:</p> \[\boldsymbol{g_{z}} = \lim_{\Delta z \to 0} \frac {U(x,y,z+ \Delta z)-U(x,y,z)}{\Delta z}\] <p>Because the computation in <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Python</code> is discrete, I choose $ \Delta z $ as small as possible, close to 0. The field could also be computed directly with [4]:</p> \[\boldsymbol{g_{z}} = -G \frac{m \Delta z} {r^3}\] <p>but computing the derivative ourselves is a good exercise.</p> <p>Let’s put it into practice:</p> <h4 id="1-import-the-modules-and-define-the-constants">1. Import the modules and define the constants</h4> <p>I use the mass and depth below; feel free to change them.</p> <div class="language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><span class="kn">import</span> <span class="n">numpy</span> <span class="k">as</span> <span class="n">np</span>
<span class="kn">import</span> <span class="n">matplotlib.pyplot</span> <span class="k">as</span> <span class="n">plt</span>

<span class="c1"># define some constants
</span><span class="n">G</span> <span class="o">=</span> <span class="mf">6.67408e-11</span> <span class="c1"># gravitational constant
</span><span class="n">m</span> <span class="o">=</span> <span class="mf">1e12</span>  <span class="c1"># mass of a point mass in kg
</span><span class="n">d</span> <span class="o">=</span> <span class="o">-</span><span class="mi">500</span> <span class="c1"># depth of a point mass in m
</span></code></pre></div></div> <p>The mass is 10^12 kg and the depth 500 m, matching the example in Gravity and Magnetic Exploration.</p> <h4 id="2-create-the-coordinates">2. Create the coordinates</h4> <p>First the coordinate ranges:</p> <div class="language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><span class="c1"># create x, y, z sparse
</span><span class="n">x</span> <span class="o">=</span> <span class="n">np</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nf">arange</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="o">-</span><span class="mi">1500</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="mi">1500</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="mi">50</span><span class="p">)</span>
<span class="n">y</span> <span class="o">=</span> <span class="n">np</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nf">arange</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="o">-</span><span class="mi">1500</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="mi">1500</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="mi">50</span><span class="p">)</span>
<span class="n">z</span> <span class="o">=</span> <span class="n">np</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nf">arange</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="o">-</span><span class="mi">500</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="mi">10</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="mi">10</span><span class="p">)</span>
</code></pre></div></div> <p>The ranges are -1500 to 1450 m for <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">x</code> and <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">y</code>, and -500 to 0 m for <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">z</code>. Then we build a <em>grid</em> as a <em>3D array</em>:</p> <div class="language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><span class="c1"># create X, Y, Z grid
</span><span class="n">X</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="n">Y</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="n">Z</span> <span class="o">=</span> <span class="n">np</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nf">meshgrid</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">x</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="n">y</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="n">z</span><span class="p">)</span>
</code></pre></div></div> <p>Geophysicists will know this step as <em>gridding</em>.</p> <h4 id="3-define-a-function-for-the-potential--u-">3. Define a function for the potential $ U $</h4> <p>A simple function to compute the gravitational potential:</p> <div class="language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><span class="c1"># define function for gravitational potential
</span><span class="k">def</span> <span class="nf">grav_potential</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">xi</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="n">yi</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="n">zi</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="n">m</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="n">d</span><span class="p">):</span>
 <span class="sh">"</span><span class="s">Calculate grav potential in a point in 3D space due to mass m in d depth, ex: grav_potential(X, Y, Z, 100, 5)</span><span class="sh">"</span>
 <span class="n">r</span> <span class="o">=</span> <span class="n">np</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nf">sqrt</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">xi</span><span class="o">**</span><span class="mi">2</span> <span class="o">+</span> <span class="n">yi</span><span class="o">**</span><span class="mi">2</span> <span class="o">+</span> <span class="p">(</span><span class="n">zi</span><span class="o">-</span><span class="n">d</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="o">**</span><span class="mi">2</span><span class="p">)</span>
 <span class="n">r</span><span class="p">[</span><span class="n">r</span><span class="o">==</span><span class="mi">0</span><span class="p">]</span><span class="o">=</span><span class="n">np</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="n">nan</span> <span class="c1">#avoid dividing by zero
</span> <span class="n">pot</span> <span class="o">=</span> <span class="n">G</span> <span class="o">*</span> <span class="n">m</span> <span class="o">/</span> <span class="n">r</span>
 <span class="k">return</span> <span class="n">pot</span>
</code></pre></div></div> <p>Note the <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">zi-d</code> term in the distance to the <em>point mass</em>. <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">r[r==0]=np.nan</code> removes the value at <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">r=0</code> (at the point mass itself) to avoid a division by zero (<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">ZeroDivisionError</code>).</p> <h4 id="4-define-the-derivative-with-respect-to--z--the-field">4. Define the derivative with respect to $ z $ (the field)</h4> <p>We differentiate the gravitational potential into the $ z $ component of the gravity field with the derivative formula above [3].</p> <div class="language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><span class="c1">#define function for derivative
</span><span class="n">dz</span> <span class="o">=</span> <span class="mf">0.000001</span>
<span class="k">def</span> <span class="nf">grav_field</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Xi</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="n">Yi</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="n">Zi</span><span class="p">):</span>
    <span class="sh">"</span><span class="s">Calculate derivative in certain component, ex: grav_field(X, Y, Z+dz) --&gt; Z component</span><span class="sh">"</span>
    <span class="n">field</span> <span class="o">=</span> <span class="p">((</span><span class="nf">grav_potential</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Xi</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="n">Yi</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="n">Zi</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="n">m</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="n">d</span><span class="p">)</span> <span class="o">-</span> <span class="nf">grav_potential</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">X</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="n">Y</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="n">Z</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="n">m</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="n">d</span><span class="p">))</span> <span class="o">/</span> <span class="n">dz</span><span class="p">)</span>
    <span class="k">return</span> <span class="n">field</span>
</code></pre></div></div> <p>I use $ \Delta z $ = <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">dz = 0.000001</code>, which I consider close enough to <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">0</code>.</p> <h4 id="5-compute-the-derivative-with-respect-to--z-">5. Compute the derivative with respect to $ z $</h4> <p>For another component, move <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">dz</code> to that coordinate. The result is multiplied by <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">1e5</code> to get a convenient colour scale.</p> <div class="language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><span class="c1"># call the derivative
</span><span class="n">grav_z</span> <span class="o">=</span> <span class="nf">grav_field</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">X</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="n">Y</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="n">Z</span><span class="o">+</span><span class="n">dz</span><span class="p">)</span> <span class="o">*</span> <span class="mf">1e5</span>
</code></pre></div></div> <p>The factor <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">1e5</code> is shown again in the colour bar label.</p> <h4 id="6-plot-at-depth-0">6. Plot at depth 0</h4> <p>For another depth, replace the index <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-1</code>; <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">z</code> runs from -500 to 0 in steps of 10. For example, -450 m is index <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">5</code>, -500 m is index <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">0</code> and -10 m is index <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-2</code>.</p> <div class="language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><span class="n">plt</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nf">contourf</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">X</span><span class="p">[:,:,</span><span class="o">-</span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="p">],</span> <span class="n">Y</span><span class="p">[:,:,</span><span class="o">-</span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="p">],</span> <span class="n">grav_z</span><span class="p">[:,:,</span><span class="o">-</span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="p">])</span>
</code></pre></div></div> <h4 id="7-finally-add-the-plotting-parameters">7. Finally, add the <em>plotting</em> parameters</h4> <div class="language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><span class="n">plt</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nf">colorbar</span><span class="p">()</span>
<span class="n">plt</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nf">text</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mi">1600</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="mi">1600</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="sh">"</span><span class="s">x 10^-5 m/s^2</span><span class="sh">"</span><span class="p">)</span>
<span class="n">plt</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nf">xlabel</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="sh">"</span><span class="s">Easting (m)</span><span class="sh">"</span><span class="p">)</span>
<span class="n">plt</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nf">ylabel</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="sh">"</span><span class="s">Northing (m)</span><span class="sh">"</span><span class="p">)</span>
<span class="n">plt</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nf">title</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="sh">"</span><span class="s">Gravitational field in Z comp at 0 m of a point </span><span class="se">\n</span><span class="s"> mass (1e12 kg) located at 500 m depth</span><span class="sh">"</span><span class="p">)</span>
<span class="n">plt</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nf">show</span><span class="p">()</span>
</code></pre></div></div> <p>And the result: <img src="/static/images/posts/grav_z.jpg" alt="$ g_{z} $" class="img-fluid"/></p> <p>To check the result, you can set <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">m</code> to the mass of the Earth and <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">d</code> to the Earth’s radius. This ignores the Earth’s curvature, but is enough to check the order of magnitude of the gravity field. Thank you for reading.</p>]]></content><author><name></name></author><category term="geophysics"/><category term="gravity,"/><category term="python"/><summary type="html"><![CDATA[Using Python to plot the gravity field of a point mass, reproduced from Gravity and Magnetic Exploration - Principles, Practices, and Applications (Hinze et al., 2013)]]></summary></entry></feed>